<h3>Circuit Schedule General Notes.</h3>
<p>
The lighting design drawings show the lighting intent only. For technical coordination see Electrical Engineering and Architectural documents.
</p>
<p>The lighting layouts must be read in conjunction with the Circuit Schedule, Fixture Specification, and, their associated notes.
</p>

<p>TSee Circuit Schedule for information about control of the individual sub circuits.</p>

<p>See Fixture specification for information about the light fixtures and whether fixtures require accessories such as transformers or ballasts.</p>

<p>Check Interior Design and Architectural elevations for confirmation mounting heights of fixtures and switches.</p>

<p>All wiring to be in accordance with local codes and as instructed by the local Electrical Engineer.</p>

<p>Plug in table lamps and floor lamps are to be fed from dedicated lighting outlet sockets, typically located at skirting height or as shown on ID elevations.</p>

<p>Provide data cable from Lighting Control Panel (LCP) to dimmer racks, in accordance with specification from dimmer manufacturer.</p>

<p>Where sub-circuits are fed from dimmer rack, sub-circuit wiring is to be run from fixtures directly back to dimmer rack for supply from the dimmer rack.</p>

<p>Downlight holes to be cut with professional circular hole-cutter. No ceiling framing or insulation material within 50mm of downlight holes.</p>
<p>Ensure transformers and light fixtures are not subjected to over heating caused by proximity of thermal insulation material.</p>
<p>All extra low voltage secondary cables (typically from ELV transformers to light fixtures) will be arranged and sized to ensure less than 5% voltage drop across the secondary cable, and, to ensure correct load protection of secondary cables.</p>
<p>Specialist lighting subcontractors should be used where required. This may include, but is not limited to, supply and installation of cold cathode lighting equipment and dimmer system equipment</p>

<p><strong>Explanation of Circuit Schedule Document.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Circuit Ref</strong> This indicates the name for the sub circuit. All cable ends must be labelled with the Circuit Reference number in such a way as the label will stay in place and be legible after the cable has been fitted off.</p>

<p><strong>Fitting Qty & Fitting Ref</strong> This indicates the quantity and type of fixtures connected to each subcircuit. See the "Fixture Specification" for a description of the components required to make up each Fixture.</p>

<p><strong>Power</strong> The wattage of the lights on the sub circuit.</p>
<p><strong>Load Type</strong> This indicates the type of load connected:</p>
<p>
	TRX = extra low voltage fixture with transformer.<br>
	TUNG = regular line voltage Tungsten Load.<br>
	FLUO = fluorescent load.<br>
	EFLUO = dimmable fluorescent load.<br>
</p>

<p><strong>Comments</strong> This describes the lights or the purpose of the lights on the subcircuit.</p>

<p><strong>Load</strong> This indicates the Amperage of the similar fixture types within the subcircuit.</p>

<p><strong>Group Load</strong> This indicates the total Amperage of the subcircuit.</p>
<p><strong>FedFrom</strong> This indicates from where the sub circuit is to be fed and how it is controlled/switched:</p>

