<p><strong>Phase</strong> If included, this indicates the phase from which the given subcircuit will be fed, if the dimmers are hooked up according to the dimmer hookup list.</p>

<p><strong>Area/Channel</strong>  If included, this indicates the control channel for setting levels. Area indicates from which area the subcircuit is controlled, and Channel indicates the channel number within the area. eg. 1/03 indicates the subcircuit is controlled as channel 3 in area 1. This is only important for setting levels and has no effect on installation<br>
	Use the information at "Fed From" to determine where to connect the subcircuits.
	</p>

<p><strong>General notes on the room lighting control system</strong></p>
<p>The lighting control in the guestroom should be by way of a dimmer and energy management system, not unlike the hotel room systems often used in hotels, but tailored to provide a switch control plate that the guest will find appropriate.<br>The lighting control system hardware for the room in this case will need to have modules that will switch and dim normal lamps, low voltage lamps, dimmable fluorescent lamps, and pulse width modulated LED dimming.</p>

<p>The recommended brand that will handle this combination is an economical DIN rail mounted switchboard configuration is Interel.<br>
	Due to the specific combination of channels and load types for the rooms, we will have Interel’s head office prepare a tailored proposal and bid for the system.</p>

<p>In the meantime the attached general catalogue pages, along with the sketch system schematic, should describe the conceptual system requirements in this case. And this should be suitable also for describing the performance requirements to any alternative bidder.</p>
<p>It would be prudent for the lighting control system in the suites to be of the brand and series as the system that will be used in the hotel central and public areas.</p>

